```apex
/**
 * DealEventPublisher
 * -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Publishes one Deal_Stage_Change__e platform event per Opportunity so the data
 * warehouse is notified of stage changes only for committed transactions (the
 * event is defined with "Publish After Commit").
 *
 * Contract
 *  - Returns one Database.SaveResult per input Opportunity, index-aligned with
 *    the input list, so a caller can map a failure back to the exact record.
 *  - An empty (or null) input publishes nothing and returns an empty list.
 *  - Opportunity_Id__c = the 18-character Id (Apex Id values are always 18
 *    characters, regardless of the case they were queried with),
 *    Stage__c = StageName, Amount__c = Amount.
 *  - No SOQL and no record DML: the only governor-limit consumption is the
 *    platform event publish itself.
 *  - Expects records that already have an Id (after insert / after update
 *    context, e.g. the Opportunity trigger handler). In a before-insert context
 *    Id would be null and the event would carry a null Opportunity_Id__c.
 *
 * DEVIATION FROM THE REQUESTED IMPLEMENTATION
 *  - The spec asked for "EventBus.publish for each opportunity inside the loop".
 *    That version fails the 200-record trigger batch: every EventBus.publish()
 *    call counts as one DML statement against the 150 DML-statements-per-
 *    transaction limit, so a 200-opportunity batch ends in
 *    "System.LimitException: Too many DML statements: 151" before the handler
 *    can even finish (and that is before any other DML the handler performs).
 *  - Fix: the events are still built one-per-opportunity, but they are published
 *    in chunks of MAX_EVENTS_PER_PUBLISH (100). A 200-record batch therefore
 *    costs 2 DML statements instead of 200. EventBus.publish(List<...>) returns
 *    one Database.SaveResult per submitted event, in the same order, so the
 *    results are copied back into the returned list at the original positions:
 *    the list stays index-aligned with the input and each Database.SaveResult
 *    still tells you exactly which event was accepted and which one was not.
 *  - Chunking (rather than one publish for the whole list) keeps the size of any
 *    single publish bounded, which keeps failure handling granular and keeps the
 *    publish well inside the per-transaction platform-event limits even if this
 *    method is ever called with more than a trigger batch.
 *  - A null element in the input list is not published and gets a null slot in
 *    the returned list so the index alignment is never broken. (Trigger.new
 *    never contains nulls; this is purely defensive against other callers.)
 */
public with sharing class DealEventPublisher {

    /**
     * Number of events submitted per EventBus.publish() call.
     * 100 => a full 200-record trigger batch consumes 2 DML statements.
     */
    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer MAX_EVENTS_PER_PUBLISH = 100;

    /** Static utility - never instantiated. */
    private DealEventPublisher() {
    }

    /**
     * Publishes one Deal_Stage_Change__e per Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunities Opportunities whose stage has changed. May be null or empty.
     * @return Database.SaveResult per input Opportunity, in the same order
     *         (empty list when nothing was published). A null input element
     *         yields a null result at the same index.
     */
    public static List<Database.SaveResult> publishStageChanges(List<Opportunity> opportunities) {
        // Nothing to publish: return an empty list, do not call EventBus at all.
        if (opportunities == null || opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return new List<Database.SaveResult>();
        }

        // Pre-size and pre-fill so results can be assigned by original index.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = new List<Database.SaveResult>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < opportunities.size(); i++) {
            results.add(null);
        }

        for (Integer start = 0; start < opportunities.size(); start += MAX_EVENTS_PER_PUBLISH) {
            Integer endIndex = Math.min(start + MAX_EVENTS_PER_PUBLISH, opportunities.size());

            List<Deal_Stage_Change__e> events = new List<Deal_Stage_Change__e>();
            List<Integer> eventIndexes = new List<Integer>();

            for (Integer i = start; i < endIndex; i++) {
                Opportunity opp = opportunities[i];
                if (opp == null) {
                    continue; // leave the null placeholder in place
                }
                events.add(new Deal_Stage_Change__e(
                    Opportunity_Id__c = String.valueOf(opp.Id),
                    Stage__c = opp.StageName,
                    Amount__c = opp.Amount
                ));
                eventIndexes.add(i);
            }

            if (events.isEmpty()) {
                continue;
            }

            // One publish call per chunk: one DML statement, one SaveResult per
            // event, in the same order the events were submitted.
            List<Database.SaveResult> chunkResults = EventBus.publish(events);

            for (Integer j = 0; j < chunkResults.size(); j++) {
                results[eventIndexes[j]] = chunkResults[j];
            }
        }

        return results;
    }
}
```

The failure was caused by the overflow strategy, not by the per‑case job. My previous version had a "fan‑out" Queueable enqueue *other* Queueables. Two problems:

1. **Stack depth** – chained Queueables are limited (and in test context, jobs executed by `Test.stopTest()` hit `Maximum stack depth has been reached` when they enqueue further Queueables). Snapshot/data‑cleanup bulk closes (200 cases) therefore blew up.
2. It was also needless indirection.

Fix: keep the required behaviour exactly – **one `CaseSurveyJob` per case** – whenever a transaction can enqueue them (≤ 50 jobs per transaction). For the bulk overflow path (e.g. the nightly 200‑case API cleanup) the trigger hands the same case Ids to `Database.executeBatch(..., 1)` on the same class, which now also implements `Database.Batchable<SObject>`. Scope size 1 gives the identical guarantee the requirement asks for: **each case runs in its own transaction, so one failure cannot affect the others** – without nesting async jobs. (The class must live in `CaseSurveyJob` because only the two files are deliverables.)

No nested async enqueueing happens in either path, and no case is processed twice.

File: force-app/main/default/triggers/CaseTrigger.trigger
```apex
trigger CaseTrigger on Case (after update) {
    if (!Trigger.isAfter || !Trigger.isUpdate) {
        return;
    }

    List<Id> newlyClosedCaseIds = new List<Id>();

    for (Case c : Trigger.new) {
        Case oldCase = Trigger.oldMap.get(c.Id);
        if (oldCase == null) {
            continue;
        }

        // Not closed -> closed, and the customer can actually be surveyed.
        if (c.IsClosed && !oldCase.IsClosed && c.ContactId != null) {
            newlyClosedCaseIds.add(c.Id);
        }
    }

    if (newlyClosedCaseIds.isEmpty()) {
        return;
    }

    if (newlyClosedCaseIds.size() <= CaseSurveyJob.MAX_JOBS_PER_TRANSACTION) {
        // Normal agent bulk close: one Queueable per case, one transaction per case.
        for (Id caseId : newlyClosedCaseIds) {
            System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(caseId));
        }
        return;
    }

    // Large bulk close (e.g. 200 cases via the API): a single transaction cannot
    // enqueue more than 50 Queueables, and chaining Queueables from an executing
    // Queueable is strictly limited (stack depth / test context). Use a batch with
    // scope size 1 instead: still exactly one case per transaction, so one failure
    // cannot affect the other cases.
    Database.executeBatch(new CaseSurveyJob(newlyClosedCaseIds), 1);
}
